## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to respond to production incidents with a concrete, beginner-friendly plan focusing on observable signals, containment, rollback, and validation under time pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: latency p95, error rate, queue depth, redis/backlog metrics
- Containment: throttle concurrency, pause new work, revert patch safely
- Rollback & Validation: restore previous config, synthetic tests, end-to-end checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple end-to-end validation stub
async function checkDelivery(queueClient, producer) {
  const msg = {id: 'test', payload: 'ping'};
  await producer.send(msg);
  const ok = await queueClient.awaitDeliveryAck(msg.id, 5000);
  return ok;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback trigger and rollback-on-fail thresholds?
- Which metrics would you add to distinguish transient spikes from persistent issues?

Why This Is Asked
Tests practical SRE handling of backlog storms in asynchronous, cross-region pipelines, beyond generic incident response. It emphasizes concrete controls, testability, and safe recovery.

Key Concepts
- Asynchronous pipelines (Kafka, event buses)
- DLQ and consumer lag signals
- Backpressure and throttling policies
- Idempotency and exactly-once semantics
- Canary testing and burst traffic in staging
- Observability: backlog, latency, error ratios, SLOs

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dynamic throttling for backlog-aware recovery
class ThrottleController {
  constructor(maxRate) { this.rate = maxRate; this.maxRate = maxRate; this.minRate = 0.1; }
  adjust(state) {
    if (state.backlog >= state.targetBacklog) this.rate = Math.max(this.minRate, this.rate * 0.8);
    else if (state.lag <= state.targetLag) this.rate = Math.min(this.maxRate, this.rate * 1.25);
    applyRate(state.topic, this.rate);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical incident engineering: how to fuse disparate signals, assign accountability, and make safe change decisions under pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-signal correlation across metrics/logs/traces
- SLO-aligned alert routing and ownership mapping
- Canary and synthetic testing for regression prevention
- RCA templating and rollback playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple rule mapping alerts to on-call owners
const owners = {
  payments: "team-payments",
  auth: "team-auth",
  inventory: "team-inventory"
};
function route(alert) {
  const svc = alert.tags?.service || "general";
  return owners[svc] || "oncall";
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the end-to-end runbook in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to validate the success of the rollback strategy?
